# Compiled claude-rules outputs — managed by "minsky rules compile --target
# claude-rules" (mt#2868), not Prettier. Same single/double-quote conflict as
# .claude/agents/ below: the emitter single-quotes the `paths:` flow-sequence
# so a sanitized `\[` bracket-escape survives YAML round-trip untouched
# (single-quoted YAML scalars have no backslash-escape processing); Prettier's
# markdown formatter rewrites those to double quotes (and would also collapse
# the deliberate lack of a blank line between the closing `---` and the
# generation banner, which matters for the check-generated-file-edit 5-line
# scan window — see claude-rules.ts's module doc). The compile-check guard
# (src/hooks/pre-commit.ts runRulesCompileCheck) owns staleness instead.
.claude/rules/

# Compiled agent outputs — managed by "minsky compile --target claude-agents",
# not Prettier. js-yaml emits single-quoted YAML for comma-containing scalars
# (e.g. `tools: 'Read, Bash'`) which Prettier rewrites to double quotes; the two
# can't both win, so the generator owns the format and the compile-check guard
# (src/hooks/pre-commit.ts runCompileCheck) enforces staleness instead (mt#2497).
.claude/agents/

# Compiled skill outputs — managed by "minsky compile --target claude-skills",
# not Prettier. The compiler emits the source markdown verbatim, so when a source
# uses Prettier-divergent markdown (e.g. `*italic*` vs `_italic_`), lint-staged
# would rewrite the compiled SKILL.md and the compile-check guard
# (src/hooks/pre-commit.ts runCompileCheck) would then flag it STALE — a deadlock
# that blocks the commit (mt#2555). The compile-check guard owns staleness for
# compiled skills instead.
# NOTE: .claude/skills/ is a SHARED output dir — it also holds legacy hand-authored
# SKILL.md files (no .minsky/skills/ source); those are no longer Prettier-checked
# as a side effect of this ignore. Author new skills under .minsky/skills/ (ADR-015).
.claude/skills/

# Generator output ignored by a NESTED .gitignore, which Prettier never reads (mt#3880).
# Prettier DOES consult .gitignore — `--ignore-path` defaults to [.gitignore,
# .prettierignore] — but only the one in the directory it is run from, i.e. the repo root.
# Every tree below is ignored by a per-subdirectory .gitignore the root file says nothing
# about, so `format:check` walked them and failed on any machine that had run the
# corresponding generator. Root .prettierignore is Prettier's documented answer for exactly
# this (https://prettier.io/docs/ignore).
#
# Two anchoring modes below, on purpose. A pattern whose ONLY separator is the trailing one
# (`target/`, `.astro/`) matches at ANY depth; a pattern with a separator in the middle
# (`/cockpit-tray/src-tauri/gen/`) is anchored to this file's directory, i.e. the repo root.
# The leading slash on the anchored two is redundant — a mid-string separator already anchors
# — but it is written explicitly because the distinction is easy to misread, and PR #2756's
# review did misread it.
#
# The depth-agnostic pair is a deliberate tradeoff, so state its cost: a directory literally
# named `target/` or `.astro/` ANYWHERE in the repo is skipped, even if it holds authored
# source. That is the price of covering a second crate or a second Astro site without another
# entry here, and it is worth it because three of these trees appeared in three days. If you
# ever add a hand-authored `target/` or `.astro/`, it will be silently unformatted — anchor
# the generated one instead and drop the broad pattern. (The same caveat already applies to
# `dist/`, `out/`, and `coverage/` above; this is not a new class of assumption.)
#
# Verified against `git ls-files`: zero TRACKED files match any pattern below, so nothing
# that was being checked stopped being checked.
#
# Comments must own their line — in gitignore syntax `#` only starts a comment at the
# start of a line, so a trailing one becomes part of the pattern and matches nothing.

# cargo build output, any crate (cockpit-tray/.gitignore: `src-tauri/target/`)
target/
# Astro generated types, any site (services/site/.gitignore: `.astro/`)
.astro/
# Tauri generated schemas. Anchored, not depth-agnostic: `gen/` is too common a directory
# name to claim repo-wide, unlike `target/` and `.astro/` above.
/cockpit-tray/src-tauri/gen/
